Without -E, -S or -c, the driver compiles each module to a temporary object
file and links the objects with the compiled standard library, the runtime
library and C's maths library, through Clang.

RUN: merkc -### -O2 --target=x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu %S/Inputs/empty-main.m -o prog 2>&1 \
RUN:   | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=LINK
LINK: "-fc1" "-emit-obj" "-triple" "x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu" "-O2" "-o" "[[OBJ:[^"]*empty-main-[^"]*\.o]]" "{{[^"]*}}empty-main.m"{{$}}
LINK-NEXT: "{{[^"]*}}clang{{(\.exe)?}}" "--target=x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu" "-o" "prog" "[[OBJ]]" "-L{{[^"]*}}lib" "-lmerkstd" "-lmerkrt" "-lm"{{$}}

With -fopenmp, Clang is given -fopenmp too, so it links libomp, as Flang's
driver does.
RUN: merkc -### -fopenmp --target=x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu %S/Inputs/empty-main.m -o prog 2>&1 \
RUN:   | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=OPENMP
OPENMP: "-fc1" "-emit-obj" "-fopenmp" "-triple" "x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu"
OPENMP-NEXT: "{{[^"]*}}clang{{(\.exe)?}}" "--target=x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu" "-fopenmp" "-o" "prog" "{{[^"]*}}.o" "-L{{[^"]*}}lib" "-lmerkstd" "-lmerkrt" "-lm"{{$}}

-c and -S stop after the object file and the assembly.
RUN: merkc -### -c %S/Inputs/empty-main.m 2>&1 | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=C
C: "-fc1" "-emit-obj" "{{[^"]*}}empty-main.m"{{$}}
C-NOT: clang
RUN: merkc -### -S -o out.s %S/Inputs/empty-main.m 2>&1 | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=S
S: "-fc1" "-S" "-o" "out.s" "{{[^"]*}}empty-main.m"{{$}}

Object files on the command line are linked.
RUN: merkc -### a.o b.o 2>&1 | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=OBJECTS
OBJECTS-NOT: -fc1
OBJECTS: "-o" "a.out" "a.o" "b.o" "-L{{[^"]*}}lib" "-lmerkstd" "-lmerkrt" "-lm"

The program runs, and main succeeding is exit status 0.
RUN: rm -rf %t && mkdir %t && cd %t
RUN: merkc %S/Inputs/empty-main.m -o empty-main
RUN: ./empty-main
RUN: merkc -c %S/Inputs/empty-main.m
RUN: ls empty-main.o
RUN: merkc empty-main.o -o linked
RUN: ./linked
RUN: merkc -S -O2 %S/Inputs/empty-main.m
RUN: FileCheck %s --check-prefix=ASM < empty-main.s
ASM: empty_main.main_2_0:
ASM: merk_main:
